===Verb===
{{en-verb|head=[[take]] [[out]]|takes out|taking out|took out|taken out}}

#To [[remove]].
#:{{ux|en|Please '''take out''' the trash before the whole house starts to smell.}}
#*{{RQ:BLwnds TLdgr|I|0056}}
#*:Thanks to that penny he had just spent so recklessly [on a newspaper] he would pass a happy hour, '''taken''', for once, '''out''' of his anxious, despondent, miserable self. It irritated him shrewdly to know that these moments of respite from carking care would not be shared with his poor wife, with careworn, troubled Ellen.
#To [[escort]] someone on a [[date]].
#:{{ux|en|Let me '''take''' you '''out''' for dinner.}}
#{{lb|en|idiomatic}} To [[immobilize]] with [[force]].
#{{lb|en|slang|idiomatic}} To [[kill]] or [[destroy]].
#{{lb|en|transitive}} To obtain by application by a legal or other official process.
#:{{ux|en|'''take out''' a loan;&emsp; '''take out''' medical insurance;&emsp; '''take out''' a membership;&emsp; '''take out''' a patent}}
#*{{quote-news|year=2012|date=April 19|author=Josh Halliday|work=the Guardian
|title=[http://www.guardian.co.uk/technology/2012/apr/19/free-speech-haven-lawless-cesspool Free speech haven or lawless cesspool – can the internet be civilised?]
|passage=Southwark council, which '''took out''' the injunction against Matt, believes YouTube has become the "new playground" for gang members.}}

